# Definition of done — {{ project_name }}

A change is done when it passes all nine pillars. A pillar that does not apply says so in one line, with the
reason; an unasked question and a genuinely empty answer must not look the same.

| # | Pillar | Done when |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | **Documented** | The architecture entry explains the choice and the alternatives rejected; the runbook has the real commands; API and host lists are current. |
| 2 | **Placed** | The architecture model shows it; a non-obvious flow has a sequence diagram. |
| 3 | **Demonstrated** | A demo was actually run, end to end, including a negative case; a UI has eyes-on steps. A demo written but not run is not done. |
| 4 | **Reversible** | Anything a demo creates has a teardown, or the demo says it is read-only. |
| 5 | **Tracked** | The backlog and the issue tracker agree. |
| 6 | **Operationally durable** | It runs on a schedule or trigger, is monitored (freshness and failure), and its secrets are restorable. Say whether a check runs by hand, in CI or on a timer, and why. |
| 7 | **Scanned** | Security and quality scans ran on the change; every new high or critical is fixed or accepted with a written reason. |
| 8 | **Cascaded** | Everything that had to move because this moved, did: callers, docs, diagrams, inventories. |
| 9 | **Recoverable** | Every piece of state has a backup and a restore that was actually tested, with the date recorded. |

## For a tool that deploys nothing

A repo guard or local script has no deployment, so pillars 2, 6 and 9 shrink: its runbook section is the home,
the CLI walkthrough is the whole demo (and must show the guard failing, with its exit code), and DR is "no state"
unless it writes some. Exit codes are part of the contract: `1` the estate has a defect, `2` the check could not
run.
